1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,457
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,457 --> 00:00:04,602
Episode 5.

3
00:00:04,602 --> 00:00:07,343
Pancakes with Tiny Rooms.

4
00:00:07,343 --> 00:00:10,184
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,184 --> 00:00:14,261
What helped make the tiny spaces inside the pancake?

6
00:00:14,261 --> 00:00:15,752
Ready?

7
00:00:15,752 --> 00:00:17,926
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,926 --> 00:00:21,562
Today the cooking club is making pancakes.

9
00:00:21,562 --> 00:00:24,460
An adult handles the hot pan.

10
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:29,176
Maya reads the recipe, and Leo measures the ingredients.

11
00:00:29,176 --> 00:00:31,321
Pip wants to help.

12
00:00:31,321 --> 00:00:35,639
I have added an important ingredient, he announces.

13
00:00:35,639 --> 00:00:37,713
What ingredient?

14
00:00:37,713 --> 00:00:39,644
asks Maya.

15
00:00:39,644 --> 00:00:41,690
Encouragement.

16
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:44,147
You are doing very well.

17
00:00:44,147 --> 00:00:49,275
They mix flour, milk, an egg, and a little baking powder.

18
00:00:49,275 --> 00:00:52,599
Leo sees bubbles appearing in the batter.

19
00:00:52,599 --> 00:00:54,474
Is it breathing?

20
00:00:54,474 --> 00:00:56,377
he asks.

21
00:00:56,377 --> 00:00:59,133
No, says Ms. Park.

22
00:00:59,133 --> 00:01:02,343
The baking powder helps make gas.

23
00:01:02,343 --> 00:01:05,028
That gas forms bubbles.

24
00:01:05,028 --> 00:01:09,090
When the first pancake is ready, she cuts it open.

25
00:01:09,090 --> 00:01:12,073
Inside are many tiny holes.

26
00:01:12,073 --> 00:01:14,076
It has rooms!

27
00:01:14,076 --> 00:01:15,980
says Maya.

28
00:01:15,980 --> 00:01:17,840
Can I move in?

29
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:19,701
Pip asks.

30
00:01:19,701 --> 00:01:23,579
Only if you become much smaller, Leo says.

31
00:01:23,579 --> 00:01:29,275
They compare the fluffy pancake with a thin piece that has fewer little spaces.

32
00:01:29,275 --> 00:01:31,974
The textures feel different.

33
00:01:31,974 --> 00:01:37,428
Maya writes a note for the club: Tiny bubbles can make a big difference.

34
00:01:37,428 --> 00:01:41,732
Leo wants to add ten times as much baking powder.

35
00:01:41,732 --> 00:01:45,426
Then we could make the tallest pancake in the world.

36
00:01:45,426 --> 00:01:48,039
Ms. Park shakes her head.

37
00:01:48,039 --> 00:01:50,667
More is not always better.

38
00:01:50,667 --> 00:01:54,034
Recipes use amounts that work together.

39
00:01:54,034 --> 00:01:56,235
Pip makes a menu.

40
00:01:56,235 --> 00:02:02,840
Today's special: a pancake with many rooms, no windows, and a very short life.

41
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,127
Why a short life?

42
00:02:05,127 --> 00:02:07,244
Maya asks.

43
00:02:07,244 --> 00:02:10,099
Because Leo is about to eat it.

44
00:02:10,099 --> 00:02:13,139
Now, a little more to discover.

45
00:02:13,139 --> 00:02:20,823
Baking powder can release carbon dioxide gas when it becomes wet, and many kinds release more when heated.

46
00:02:20,823 --> 00:02:23,451
Bubbles help batter rise.

47
00:02:23,451 --> 00:02:28,721
As the pancake cooks, its structure firms up around tiny spaces.

48
00:02:28,721 --> 00:02:33,125
Those spaces help make the pancake feel light and soft.

49
00:02:33,125 --> 00:02:36,093
Let us hear the main idea again.

50
00:02:36,093 --> 00:02:40,355
The cooking club found tiny holes inside a pancake.

51
00:02:40,355 --> 00:02:43,892
Gas bubbles helped create those spaces.

52
00:02:43,892 --> 00:02:48,707
They followed a recipe instead of simply adding more baking powder.

53
00:02:48,707 --> 00:02:53,210
Pip supplied encouragement, but no real ingredients.

54
00:02:53,210 --> 00:02:56,321
Here are three useful expressions.

55
00:02:56,321 --> 00:02:59,588
You can listen, or say them with me.

56
00:02:59,588 --> 00:03:02,215
Would you like some pancakes?

57
00:03:02,215 --> 00:03:04,843
Would you like some pancakes?

58
00:03:04,843 --> 00:03:07,244
Yes, please.

59
00:03:07,244 --> 00:03:09,545
They look delicious.

60
00:03:09,545 --> 00:03:11,946
Yes, please.

61
00:03:11,946 --> 00:03:14,247
They look delicious.

62
00:03:14,247 --> 00:03:16,661
Let me read the recipe.

63
00:03:16,661 --> 00:03:19,076
Let me read the recipe.

64
00:03:19,076 --> 00:03:21,789
Remember our listening question?

65
00:03:21,789 --> 00:03:25,866
What helped make the tiny spaces inside the pancake?

66
00:03:25,866 --> 00:03:28,096
Here is the answer.

67
00:03:28,096 --> 00:03:33,238
Gas bubbles in the batter helped make the spaces as the pancake cooked.

68
00:03:33,238 --> 00:03:37,059
And here is a question just for your imagination.

69
00:03:37,059 --> 00:03:41,193
What other foods have little holes or bubbles inside them?

70
00:03:41,193 --> 00:03:44,446
There is more than one possible answer.

71
00:03:44,446 --> 00:03:47,840
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
